district fundamentals with incumbent McGuire running", "Polls show a tightening race, from 6 points to 1 point in recent surveys", "Perriello holds a 2-1 cash-on-hand advantage, rare for a challenger here", "Raters split between Likely R and Lean R"], "rationale": "The district's R+6 lean and McGuire's incumbency still favor Republicans, and most raters call the race Likely or Lean R. But recent polling has narrowed to a single point in one survey, Perriello's strong fundraising gives him a real financial edge, and markets price his chances near three in ten, so the forecast sits a few points inside the Republican lean.", "watch": "Any further polling movement toward Perriello, or outside spending against McGuire, could push this into genuine tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent John McGuire (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 5th District leans Republican by about six points, and first-term incumbent John McGuire is running for reelection after surviving a contested primary. Democrat Tom Perriello, a former congressman and diplomat, is making a serious bid, and an independent candidate is also on the ballot.\n\nThe race has drawn unusual attention for a district this red. Polling in late September showed McGuire ahead by six points in one survey and by a single point in another, and headlines describe a tightening contest. Perriello has raised nearly as much as McGuire and holds a large cash advantage, while surrogates have campaigned for him in Charlottesville. Ratings from professional forecasters split between Likely R and Lean R, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a three-in-ten chance. The seat remains favored for McGuire, but not comfortably.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1914, "margin": -5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2771, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2238, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2476, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0